cat > "$SIM_DIR/discussion.json" << 'DISCUSSION_EOF'
{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "assessment_mode_intentional": true,
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Compelling market timing and a rare product-quality team for this category, but the 10-year platform thesis is not yet articulated and the fund's portfolio conflicts with ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro need full resolution at the IC level before I can advocate for this investment."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Strong early traction and elite CAC payback, but the GTM motion is a black box — both co-founders are technical and I need to see the customer acquisition playbook before I can believe this growth is scalable beyond founder hustle. Portfolio conflict must be resolved first."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Unit economics are best-in-class at seed stage, but the LTV/CAC thesis rests entirely on an unvalidated 3-year retention assumption. No cohort data, no burn rate disclosure, and the 33x trailing ARR multiple is defensible only if growth is durable. Plus, fund-fit conflict requires legal review."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Portfolio Conflict — The Elephant in the Room",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Let's just name it before we go any further. We hold ServiceTitan, Jobber, AND Housecall Pro. All three are active investments in field service SaaS for SMB trades. Cadence is operating in the same category, targeting the same customer. Any one of these would normally prompt a conflict review. All three together? That's not a nuance — that's a structural problem. I don't see how we can even have this conversation without legal weighing in first."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I hear you, but let's be precise about what 'conflict' means here. ServiceTitan is moving upmarket into commercial and enterprise. Jobber is a horizontal tool that serves dozens of verticals. Housecall Pro is focused on home services residential. If Cadence has a distinct ICP — say, sub-25-employee specialty trades operators — there may be a real segmentation argument. The market is large enough that multiple players can coexist at different tiers. The conflict concern is real, but I don't think it's automatically blocking without understanding exactly where Cadence is positioned."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The segmentation argument is worth exploring, but let's be honest about what we're really asking: are we comfortable sitting on the board of Cadence, sharing strategic insights, and making introductions — while holding active positions in three direct competitors? Even if the ICP argument holds today, Cadence at $10M ARR will look a lot more like a Jobber or Housecall Pro competitor than it does at $480K ARR. The LP optics alone are problematic. We need to table this and get a legal opinion before we proceed. If counsel clears it, great — but that's the prerequisite for everything else."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Agreed. Let's set the conflict question aside as a process gate — not a deal-killer in principle, but a mandatory check before we can commit. If legal comes back with a clear path, the business fundamentals are interesting enough to warrant real diligence. If it's blocking, we pass with a referral. Either way, we don't move forward without resolution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Traction Quality and Unit Economics",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Let me put some numbers on the table. $40K MRR, 120 customers, 18% MoM growth over four months. $1,200 CAC, 80% gross margin, $333 ARPU. The CAC payback works out to about 4.5 months — that is genuinely best-in-class for SMB SaaS. The problem is everything beyond that requires assumptions we haven't validated. The LTV calculation floats on a 3-year retention assumption with zero cohort data to back it up. If monthly churn is 3%, LTV drops by roughly a third. At 5% churn — common in price-sensitive SMB — the LTV/CAC story starts to look ordinary. I need real cohort curves before I trust the unit economics story."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Four months of 18% MoM growth is not a statistical anomaly. That's a product that people are finding and paying for. And at $333 ARPU, these are not free trials or design partners — real SMB owners are taking cash out of pocket for this tool. That's always been the hardest hurdle in SMB SaaS. The market timing argument I'd make is that post-COVID labor shortages have made scheduling and dispatch genuinely painful in ways they weren't before. Trades businesses are scrambling to manage 20% fewer crew members across the same job volume. That's a structural pull, not a marketing push."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I agree the traction is real, but let me push on the acquisition side. $1,200 CAC is excellent — if the methodology is clean. How are they computing it? If it's fully-loaded — including founder time, any marketing spend, events — that's impressive. If it's just ad spend and they're ignoring founder hours, we should restate it. I've seen founders at this stage report $800 CAC when the real number including all-in costs is closer to $3,000. I want to see the CAC calculation methodology and channel breakdown before I celebrate it."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "That's exactly right. And one more thing on the valuation: $16M pre on $480K ARR is 33x trailing. That's above the median seed multiple for SMB SaaS — I'd say 15-25x is more typical. It's defensible if the growth rate holds, but if they slow to 8-10% MoM over the next six months as the founder network gets saturated, the Series A math gets very tight. At 18% for another 12 months, they're at roughly $3.5M ARR — that's a comfortable Series A. At 10% MoM, they're at $1.2M ARR — fundable but not exciting. The valuation is not a deal-breaker but it's not a bargain either."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Team: Strengths, Gaps, and the Commercial Question",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I want to make the bull case on the team before we get into the gaps. Ex-Stripe engineer and ex-Notion PM is a pairing you almost never see in field service software. The incumbents — ServiceTitan, Jobber, Housecall Pro — were all built by domain operators who understood the trades but had limited product sophistication. That's why they all look like they were designed in 2015. Cadence potentially builds what trades workers actually want to use, not what a former HVAC dispatcher thinks they need. The Notion PLG instinct combined with Stripe-grade reliability is a real product moat if executed well."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I've seen this movie. Two brilliant technical founders, amazing product, grows to $2M ARR on founder network, then they try to hire their first AE and watch the funnel collapse because the product sells through trust and explanation and the AE can't replicate it. The SMB trades market specifically is a word-of-mouth and local referral market. Plumbers don't read TechCrunch. They don't attend SaaS webinars. They hear about tools from other plumbers at a trade show or from their supplier rep. Who on this team knows how to build those channels? The ex-Stripe engineer is brilliant at API design. The ex-Notion PM knows PLG. Neither has sold to a 10-person plumbing operation in Ohio."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "That's fair, but isn't that what the $4M is for? They don't need to have solved the GTM at seed. They need to have found enough signal that the product works. The commercial question is a hire. What I want to know is whether they know they need to make that hire and whether they have the self-awareness to bring in someone very different from themselves."
        },
