{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "assessment_mode_intentional": true,
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "Compelling market timing, strong product DNA team, and real PMF signals \u2014 but I cannot in good conscience lead this investment while the fund holds ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro. Hard pass for this fund specifically. I'd advocate for a warm referral to a fund without this conflict; Cadence deserves a board partner who can add value without compromising three existing portfolio companies."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "The traction is real \u2014 18% MoM, elite CAC payback \u2014 but the portfolio conflict is not resolvable with a legal memo. We'd be sitting on Cadence's board while holding three direct competitors. The founders would eventually find out, and it would destroy trust. Hard pass for this fund, not for the company."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "Even setting aside the business-level diligence gaps (missing cohort data, undisclosed burn rate, unvalidated LTV), the portfolio conflict alone makes this a hard pass. The formal scoring confirms two dealbreakers: portfolio conflict and competitive response risk \u2014 both tied to the same root cause of holding three active competitors. We should pass promptly and refer to a fund without this constraint."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Portfolio Conflict \u2014 The Elephant in the Room",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Let's just name it before we go any further. We hold ServiceTitan, Jobber, AND Housecall Pro. All three are active investments in field service SaaS for SMB trades. Cadence is operating in the same category, targeting the same customer. Any one of these would normally prompt a conflict review. All three together? That's not a nuance \u2014 that's a structural problem. I don't see how we can even have this conversation without legal weighing in first."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I hear you, but let's be precise about what 'conflict' means here. ServiceTitan is moving upmarket into commercial and enterprise. Jobber is a horizontal tool that serves dozens of verticals. Housecall Pro is focused on home services residential. If Cadence has a distinct ICP \u2014 say, sub-25-employee specialty trades operators \u2014 there may be a real segmentation argument. The market is large enough that multiple players can coexist at different tiers. The conflict concern is real, but I don't think it's automatically blocking without understanding exactly where Cadence is positioned."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The segmentation argument is worth exploring, but let's be honest about what we're really asking: are we comfortable sitting on the board of Cadence, sharing strategic insights, and making introductions \u2014 while holding active positions in three direct competitors? Even if the ICP argument holds today, Cadence at $10M ARR will look a lot more like a Jobber or Housecall Pro competitor than it does at $480K ARR. The LP optics alone are problematic. We need to table this and get a legal opinion before we proceed. If counsel clears it, great \u2014 but that's the prerequisite for everything else."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Agreed. Let's set the conflict question aside as a process gate \u2014 not a deal-killer in principle, but a mandatory check before we can commit. If legal comes back with a clear path, the business fundamentals are interesting enough to warrant real diligence. If it's blocking, we pass with a referral. Either way, we don't move forward without resolution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Traction Quality and Unit Economics",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Let me put some numbers on the table. $40K MRR, 120 customers, 18% MoM growth over four months. $1,200 CAC, 80% gross margin, $333 ARPU. The CAC payback works out to about 4.5 months \u2014 that is genuinely best-in-class for SMB SaaS. The problem is everything beyond that requires assumptions we haven't validated. The LTV calculation floats on a 3-year retention assumption with zero cohort data to back it up. If monthly churn is 3%, LTV drops by roughly a third. At 5% churn \u2014 common in price-sensitive SMB \u2014 the LTV/CAC story starts to look ordinary. I need real cohort curves before I trust the unit economics story."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Four months of 18% MoM growth is not a statistical anomaly. That's a product that people are finding and paying for. And at $333 ARPU, these are not free trials or design partners \u2014 real SMB owners are taking cash out of pocket for this tool. That's always been the hardest hurdle in SMB SaaS. The market timing argument I'd make is that post-COVID labor shortages have made scheduling and dispatch genuinely painful in ways they weren't before. Trades businesses are scrambling to manage 20% fewer crew members across the same job volume. That's a structural pull, not a marketing push."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I agree the traction is real, but let me push on the acquisition side. $1,200 CAC is excellent \u2014 if the methodology is clean. How are they computing it? If it's fully-loaded \u2014 including founder time, any marketing spend, events \u2014 that's impressive. If it's just ad spend and they're ignoring founder hours, we should restate it. I've seen founders at this stage report $800 CAC when the real number including all-in costs is closer to $3,000. I want to see the CAC calculation methodology and channel breakdown before I celebrate it."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "That's exactly right. And one more thing on the valuation: $16M pre on $480K ARR is 33x trailing. That's above the median seed multiple for SMB SaaS \u2014 I'd say 15-25x is more typical. It's defensible if the growth rate holds, but if they slow to 8-10% MoM over the next six months as the founder network gets saturated, the Series A math gets very tight. At 18% for another 12 months, they're at roughly $3.5M ARR \u2014 that's a comfortable Series A. At 10% MoM, they're at $1.2M ARR \u2014 fundable but not exciting. The valuation is not a deal-breaker but it's not a bargain either."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Team: Strengths, Gaps, and the Commercial Question",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I want to make the bull case on the team before we get into the gaps. Ex-Stripe engineer and ex-Notion PM is a pairing you almost never see in field service software. The incumbents \u2014 ServiceTitan, Jobber, Housecall Pro \u2014 were all built by domain operators who understood the trades but had limited product sophistication. That's why they all look like they were designed in 2015. Cadence potentially builds what trades workers actually want to use, not what a former HVAC dispatcher thinks they need. The Notion PLG instinct combined with Stripe-grade reliability is a real product moat if executed well."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I've seen this movie. Two brilliant technical founders, amazing product, grows to $2M ARR on founder network, then they try to hire their first AE and watch the funnel collapse because the product sells through trust and explanation and the AE can't replicate it. The SMB trades market specifically is a word-of-mouth and local referral market. Plumbers don't read TechCrunch. They don't attend SaaS webinars. They hear about tools from other plumbers at a trade show or from their supplier rep. Who on this team knows how to build those channels? The ex-Stripe engineer is brilliant at API design. The ex-Notion PM knows PLG. Neither has sold to a 10-person plumbing operation in Ohio."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "That's fair, but isn't that what the $4M is for? They don't need to have solved the GTM at seed. They need to have found enough signal that the product works. The commercial question is a hire. What I want to know is whether they know they need to make that hire and whether they have the self-awareness to bring in someone very different from themselves."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The hire is the right question. But here's the financial implication: today their CAC is $1,200, presumably because the founders are closing deals personally. A good SMB SaaS AE costs $120-150K all-in. Add a head of sales at $180K, some marketing spend, trade show budget \u2014 your fully-loaded S&M structure goes up materially. CAC on that basis could easily double or triple. I need to understand what their hiring plan is and how it affects the unit economics before I can get comfortable with the financial model."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Market Size and Long-Term Platform Thesis",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Here's the question I keep coming back to: is Cadence building a scheduling tool, or is it building the financial operating system for the trades economy? ServiceTitan understood this \u2014 they started with scheduling and systematically expanded into payments, financing, supply chain, and inventory. At $333 ARPU, you need roughly 25,000 customers to hit $100M ARR. That's achievable. But the really interesting path is ARPU expansion: if Cadence adds embedded payments, crew financing, or customer financing for big ticket jobs, the ARPU could go to $1,000-2,000/month without adding complexity. The ex-Stripe founder has the mental model for this. I want to hear whether they're thinking about it."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "ServiceTitan took ten years and $1.6 billion in venture capital to build that financial layer. I'm not saying it's impossible \u2014 but the comparison makes me a little nervous. What I want to see is a clear explanation of how they win the next 1,000 customers, not the 25,000. What does the go-to-market look like in year two? What channels, what partnerships, what vertical focus? The platform vision is interesting but it's downstream of survival."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I'll add one data point. The field service management market is legitimately large \u2014 ServiceTitan alone is valued at $9.5 billion. Jobber is a unicorn. Housecall Pro raised at a significant valuation. So the market can support multiple large outcomes. The question is whether Cadence captures a differentiated segment or eventually becomes a feature that one of these incumbents acquires. At $4M on $16M pre, the acquisition option is not terrible \u2014 a $60-80M outcome at 10x ARR would return the fund. But I don't think that's the vision here, and I don't think it's how the founders are thinking about it."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Agreed \u2014 I'm not looking to underwrite an acqui-hire. What I want from diligence is a founder who can articulate why Cadence is still an independent company in 2031, what the moat looks like at scale, and what the platform looks like beyond scheduling. If they can tell me that story credibly, and the cohort data holds up, I'm a yes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Verdict and Diligence Plan",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "My position: conditional more_diligence. The business shows real PMF signals \u2014 the growth rate and unit economics are better than most of what I see at seed. But I have three things I need before I can commit: one, legal resolution on the portfolio conflict; two, full customer acquisition breakdown by channel so I can assess GTM repeatability; three, cohort retention curves so I can validate the LTV thesis. If those three come back clean, I'd move to invest."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Same verdict, different priorities. My must-haves: cohort retention data \u2014 it's the single most important number in the whole story. Burn rate and a 18-month financial model so I can stress-test the Series A path. Customer concentration breakdown. And the portfolio conflict legal review is table stakes. On valuation: I'd prefer to negotiate to $13-14M pre given the traction stage and the comparable universe, but $16M is not a walkaway if the diligence clears."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I'm aligned on more_diligence. Beyond the financial diligence, I want one substantive founder conversation focused on product vision: what does Cadence look like at scale, what is the compounding thesis, and are they thinking about ARPU expansion into financial services? If the founders can answer that question with conviction and the numbers check out, this is the kind of seed bet I get excited about. Trades digitization is a decade-long wave and this is the right team archetype to surf it."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Let's be efficient. Portfolio conflict legal review happens immediately \u2014 that's the gating item. In parallel, we send the founders a structured diligence request: cohort data, P&L, customer acquisition breakdown, customer concentration. Reference calls with five customers across different verticals. And we schedule a second founder session focused on GTM plan and hiring roadmap. Two-week timeline. If everything clears, we reconvene for a final vote."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "After reviewing the formal scoring, I have to update my position. The portfolio conflict isn't just a 'more diligence' item \u2014 it's a dealbreaker for this fund specifically. We hold three direct competitors. Even if Cadence is a great company (and the early evidence suggests it might be), we cannot lead this round without compromising our fiduciary duty to ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro. My recommendation is a hard pass for this fund, with a warm referral to funds without this conflict. Cadence deserves a lead investor who can actually be a constructive board member."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "consensus_verdict": "hard_pass",
  "key_concerns": [
    "Portfolio conflict (DEALBREAKER for this fund): active positions in ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro \u2014 all three are direct competitors; legal review would confirm this as a blocking conflict, making this a hard pass for this specific fund",
    "Cohort retention data entirely absent: the LTV/CAC thesis rests on an unvalidated 3-year retention assumption; if monthly churn is 3-5%, the unit economics story changes materially",
    "GTM repeatability unproven: both co-founders are technical; no channel breakdown, no evidence the acquisition motion scales beyond founder hustle",
    "ARPU expansion thesis and long-term platform moat not articulated: at $333 ARPU the path to $100M ARR requires either volume scale or a credible expansion into payments/financial services",
    "Valuation at 33x trailing ARR is above median seed multiple; defensible only if 18% MoM growth rate is durable"
  ],
  "diligence_requirements": [
    "Legal and LP-relations review of portfolio conflict (ServiceTitan, Jobber, Housecall Pro) \u2014 mandatory gating item before any further commitment",
    "Monthly cohort revenue retention curves for all cohorts with 6+ months of history",
    "Full customer acquisition breakdown by channel (organic, referral, outbound, events, paid) with conversion rates and CAC calculation methodology",
    "Detailed P&L and burn rate for the last 6 months including fully-loaded COGS, S&M, and R&D",
    "Customer concentration analysis: top 10 customers as % of MRR",
